 of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'back pain', 'pain', 'Hemangiomatosis', 'gas', 'Hepatic hemangioma'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.459}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.3", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with Ischemia", "posterior": 0.35, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.1", "B2.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=57%, source=both]\n\nBiliary Obstruction vs Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhagic Shock:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, fever, jaundice\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nBiliary Obstruction vs Ruptured Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, jaundice, loss of appetite\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nBiliary Obstruction vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, loss of appetite, nausea and vomiting\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, ascites, autosomal recessive inheritance, budd-chiari syndrome\n\nEctopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhagic Shock vs Ruptured Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever\n\nEctopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhagic Shock vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis, cirrhosis\n\nRuptured Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, increased urinary frequency\n  Favours second: acute hepatic failure, ascites, autosomal recessive inheritance, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n\nRelated to Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhagic Shock: abdominal ectopic pregnancy, female reproductive system disease, ovarian ectopic pregnancy\n\nRelated to Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis: hepatic vascular disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhagic Shock\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhagic Shock\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → arthralgia → Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhagic Shock"}
